Hi [~accountid:60e96a31131bf80069e1459f] and [~accountid:5aaf9ba5795d0d2a5cd93d60] yes sorry I didn’t get a chance to update the ticket last week - please see below and kindly let me know if you’d likle to discuss further before or after forwarding this feedback to the FO:

* From your analysis, should we conclude that the FO has no way to “get rid” of a driver that is unilaterally blocked on our end, even for obvious reasons shuch as multiple sexual harrassment reports from riders?

You might remember that our T&Cs provide as follows:

_4.8 The Taxi Operator shall and procures that any Drivers at its service shall use Bolt Services in good faith and in accordance with the best industry practice and will not engage in fraudulent behaviour. Bolt may immediately suspend the Taxi Operator’s and/or its Drivers’ access to the App Bolt, Bolt Accounts and Bolt Services during the investigation period if Bolt suspects there is a violation of these Terms and Conditions or fraudulent activity. The access suspension is removed as soon as the investigation disproves such suspicions._

_22.4: Bolt reserves the right to terminate the Agreement and block the TVDE Operator's access to AppBolt without any prior notice in case of a breach of the Agreement and/or any applicable laws or regulations by the TVDE Operator. If the TVDE Operator has violated the terms of the Agreement and/or any applicable laws or regulations, Bolt may, at its sole discretion, prohibit such TVDE Operator from registering a new account, or take other actions necessary to prevent such person from providing Passenger Transportation Services through the Software._

{color:#4c9aff}DLA: The situation (multiple sexual harassment reports from riders) you mentioned above can be the ground for the termination of the employment contract but, taking in consideration that the Portuguese regime is applicable, this termination can only be valid after a disciplinary dismissal by the employer. In the case you mentioned above, we think that {color}{color:#4c9aff}*the FO would have just cause (based on a serious breach of the employee’s duties which leads to practical impossibility to maintain the employment relationship) to dismiss but only after the disciplinary procedure (please note that the employer as the responsibility to prove the facts that justify the dismissal)*{color}{color:#4c9aff}.{color}

{color:#4c9aff}*The T&C are the conditions to use Bolt´s platform but they can not justify, only by themselves, the dismissal*{color}{color:#4c9aff}.{color}

* Would it be possible for instance to add some termination provision in the employment contract (between FO and driver) saying that the FO has a right to terminate the contract if the driver gets blocked by Bolt for more than 14 days?

{color:#4c9aff}DLA: The grounds for termination of an employment contact are the one mentioned below and the law says that the parties can not add any other grounds. {color}{color:#4c9aff}*Such provision would be null and void*{color}{color:#4c9aff}.{color}

* Most sexual harrasments in vehicles are reported to us, but don’t end up being reported at the Police. So we would like to understand if Bolt decides directly to suspend (or not) a driver, can this have a direct impact on the FO <> Driver Agreement?

{color:#4c9aff}DLA: These are 2 diferrent relationships. On one hand, we have the relationship between the platform (Bolt) and the FO / Driver and, on the other hand, we have the labor relationship between the FO and the driver. These are 2 different and independent relationships. {color}{color:#4c9aff}*If BOLT suspends a driver in the platform it doesn´t have an immediate and direct effect on the labor relation between the FO and the driver. Of course as a consequence of the suspension the FO can start a legal procedure against the driver to terminate the labor contract. Having said this, the suspension doesn´t have a direct effect but can have an indirect effect on the labor relation between the FO and the driver*{color}{color:#4c9aff}.{color}

